Z||000020||rockcrawl||12-08-2000||09:09 AM||||Wow, you guys are getting way too excited! This Cadillac conversion is nothing new. I don't think  bubbajoexx is trying to sell anything, he's just showing off his car like everyone else likes to do. The 4.1, 4.5, and 4.9 Cad V8s are aluminum with iron heads. The 4.9 weighs the same as a 2.8 V6. The 4.1 is throttle body injected and produces 135 hp and 180 ft/lb torque. The 4.9 uses sequential port fuel injection and makes 200 hp and 275 ft/lb torque. The 4.5 is somewhere in between. Any of these engines require the same amount of effort to install as a 3800 V6. They can usually be found much cheaper (I paid $350 for a '91 4.9, entire car, runs and drives). The 4.1 is probably not a good choice because of the low power output and reliablility problems with the early engines. If you can fabricate your own mounts and connect the wiring, you can do this swap. Chris West and Team Python both have websites with more info.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||7
Z||000021||bubbajoexx||12-08-2000||06:30 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rockcrawl:<BR><B>Wow, you guys are getting way too excited! This Cadillac conversion is nothing new. I don't think  bubbajoexx is trying to sell anything, he's just showing off his car like everyone else likes to do. The 4.1, 4.5, and 4.9 Cad V8s are aluminum with iron heads. The 4.9 weighs the same as a 2.8 V6. The 4.1 is throttle body injected and produces 135 hp and 180 ft/lb torque. The 4.9 uses sequential port fuel injection and makes 200 hp and 275 ft/lb torque. The 4.5 is somewhere in between. Any of these engines require the same amount of effort to install as a 3800 V6. They can usually be found much cheaper (I paid $350 for a '91 4.9, entire car, runs and drives). The 4.1 is probably not a good choice because of the low power output and reliablility problems with the early engines. If you can fabricate your own mounts and connect the wiring, you can do this swap. Chris West and Team Python both have websites with more info.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>better check your specs. again the 9.5 to1 engines are 175 hp 200flbs for 4.1 220 hp 265flbs 4.5 245 hp  295flbs 4.9 in canadian models most us models are 8.5 to 1 compresion do to smog legislation in the US canadian smog laws are less stringent.||||reg||1
Z||000022||rockcrawl||12-08-2000||06:38 PM||||Fine and dandy for someone living in Canada, but many of us are in the US where the 4.1 has less hp than a 2.8, making it undesireable. I guess I should have been more specific. The numbers I posted are for US cars and were given to me by the guys at Team Python and are taken directly from the Cadillac service manual.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by rockcrawl (edited 12-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000023||loafer87gt||12-08-2000||06:53 PM||||Where abouts in Canada are you? I'm in Saskatchewan, but am rally interested in the Caddy swap if its as straightforward as you say. Also, I've done some digging around and from what I've found the 9.5 Comp 4.9 was putting out 200 HP. Where did you read that the Canadian spec had 240? If you could send me off whatever installation info you have, it would be much appreciated, thanks<P>||||reg||1
